<!--
  The typed coordination engine, as a `dotnet tool` (ADR-0034 Phase 3a).

  THE FLIP HAS SINCE HAPPENED (ADR-0040 Phase D COMPLETE). This tool is no longer packaged to *shadow*
  bash — it IS the coordination client. `scripts/fsgg-coord` is now the §4.4 shim that execs this
  command, the ~7,000-line bash monolith is deleted, and the shadow, the `engine=` mode flag, and the
  fleet-divergence ledger are retired. The history below is why it was packaged BEFORE the flip; it is
  kept as the record of that ordering.

  WHY IT WAS PACKAGED FIRST, AND WHY THAT WAS *NOT* THE FLIP.

  Phase 2 shipped the shadow: both engines decide, bash's answer is returned, the disagreement is
  logged, and bash stays authoritative until that log is clean. Phase 2's exit criterion is "zero
  divergence across the LIVE FLEET for three consecutive days" — and it could never be met, because
  the engine existed only where somebody had BUILT it, which is `.github` and nowhere else. Fleet
  evidence needs the engine distributed; distribution was scheduled for Phase 3; Phase 3 was gated on
  Phase 2 exiting. The clock could never start (.github#617).

  So publishing came BEFORE flipping, and it was the only order in which the flip's evidence could
  exist. Bash stayed authoritative through Phase 3a. The single thing packaging changed was that a
  worker in a receiver repo now HAD an engine to shadow with.

  IDENTITY IS FOREVER (ADR-0003; "package identity == product identity, never rename in place"):

    PackageId        FS.GG.Coord.Cli     — the name ADR-0034 §4.1 gives it, and the right one for what
                                           it BECOMES: at the flip this is the coordination client.
    ToolCommandName  fsgg-coord-engine   — and it never changes either. At the flip, the `kit:` row
                                           `scripts/fsgg-coord` becomes a shim that EXECS this command,
                                           so the command name never has to be renamed and no tool
                                           manifest ever has to be rewritten.

  STABLE, NOT -preview. The org promoted its last `-preview` producer in July, and the shared Renovate
  preset now pins to stable (`ignoreUnstable: true`). A `-preview` tool would be INVISIBLE to Renovate
  — and Renovate-invisibility is one of the stated reasons ADR-0034 §4.4 rejected the self-contained
  binary. Shipping a tool the update fabric cannot see would defeat the point of shipping it.

  `.github` builds this FROM SOURCE and never restores it from the feed. That asymmetry is what breaks
  the publish cycle: a broken feed cannot stop the coordination tool being fixed, because the repo that
  fixes it does not depend on the feed. Only RECEIVERS consume the package.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>

    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsgg-coord-engine</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageId>
    <!-- 0.15.0 to 0.16.0 is a MINOR, and under the repository's 0.x SemVer policy that is also the
         caller-observable-change signal: there is no 1.x major available to take. Eight merged engine
         commits ship (.github#1951); #1900's squash commit consolidates four reviewed input commits.
         The cut includes a Protocol.fs wire-surface change and several independent changes
         to scheduler, claim, lint, follow-up, and done-boundary behavior:

           * #1915 (0b181f2) makes `lint` name consolidation candidates.
           * #1921 (2065040) makes BLOCKER-CLEARED respect an in-flight implementation.
           * #1901 (9d7c1a2) adds the ordered Severity ranking axis.
           * #1942 (338e9aa) corrects KIT DIGEST claim advice for skill files.
           * #1946 (5a93a3b) excludes decision items from the worker queue.
           * #1948 (4f3ffa6) derives skill views from receiver declarations.
           * #1949 (91645e2) fails closed when a marker scan is incomplete.
           * #1900/#1952 (de03135, squashing f9b067c, 7d9927e, a8466c1, and 0b722c9) adds follow-up
             audit/reconciliation, makes `done` audit residual promises, and adds the durable disposition
             boundary.

         Patch is not defensible: receivers gain command vocabulary, result vocabulary, refusal paths,
         rank behavior, and terminal behavior. #1892 (secondary-rate-limit exit contract) and #1857
         (mid-item session rotation) remain explicitly unresolved residue and are not part of this cut.

         This property is the RELEASE ACT (see scripts/check-engine-freshness.py): the engine's version
         moves only at release time, by hand, and release-coord-engine.yml evaluates it with
         `dotnet msbuild -getProperty:Version` and refuses any `coord-engine/v[version]` tag that
         disagrees. The tag is a coherence check against this line and never its source. -->
    <Version>0.18.0</Version>
    <AssemblyName>fsgg-coord-engine</AssemblyName>

    <Description>FS-GG typed coordination engine — the schedulability model behind `fsgg-coord` as one total function over a board-state snapshot, plus the typed IO layer that drives it. It IS the coordination tool (ADR-0034/ADR-0040): the `fsgg-coord` shim resolves and execs it; there is no bash left to shadow.</Description>
    <Authors>FS-GG</Authors>
    <Company>FS-GG</Company>
    <PackageTags>fsgg;coordination;scheduler;dotnet-tool;fsharp</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/FS-GG/.github/tree/main/src/FS.GG.Coord.Cli</PackageProjectUrl>

    <!-- THE RELEASE NOTES ARE WHERE THE BREAK IS ANNOUNCED, and that is the point of them here.
         nuget.org renders this on the package listing and `dotnet tool` consumers read it there. A
         changelog that said only "fixes" while a previously-accepted flag started exiting 1 would be
         the #266 shape in its purest form: the org knowing a thing, and the consumer discovering it
         from a broken script. Criterion 5 of .github#1596 asks for exactly this.

         KEEP THIS PROPERTY IN STEP WITH <Version> ABOVE. The 0.13.0 cut moved <Version> and left these
         notes reading "0.12.0 — BREAKING...", so the nuget.org listing for 0.13.0 announced the PREVIOUS
         release's break and none of its own seven commits. Nothing gates the two against each other
         (.github#1762). A consumer who reads the listing and finds the wrong release described has been
         failed by it exactly as much as one who finds nothing. -->
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.18.0 — MINOR on the 0.x line, the repository's signal for caller-observable change.

Runtime changes merged after 0.17.0 make the packaged engine match the coordination and product-board
guidance already carried by Kit 0.24.0 and the driver manifest.

REPOSITORY-SCOPED TOUCH SETS (#2011): path declarations are interpreted in their declared Repo Scope,
so identical relative paths in different repositories no longer contend accidentally.

BOARD FACTS AND DIAGNOSTICS (#2040/#2042): scheduler facts are extracted behind a typed application
boundary, overlap refusals name the reserving holder, and board drivers receive structured rate-limit
classes instead of parsing prose (#2041).

SAFE RECONCILIATION (#1984/#2000/#2037): blocker-cleared repairs fail closed when the live PR probe
cannot be read, lint covers blocked verdicts, and deliberate human parks are distinguished from stale
blockers.

COMMAND AND SCHEDULER CORRECTIONS (#1991/#1992/#1993/#2031): option ownership and rendered flag
spellings agree with the command contract, dead scan rendering is removed, and filing a single-item
parallel lane is diagnosed rather than silently presented as fan-out.

Patch is not defensible: the 0.x tool gains result vocabulary, repository-scoped scheduling semantics,
and caller-visible refusal/diagnostic behavior.

PREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.17.0):

TWO merged engine commits since 0.16.0.

REFUSAL SPELLINGS (#1573): refusal text is now derived from the command-contract flag spelling table,
including the non-long `-n` limit spelling. Contract output and a refusal can no longer silently name
different flags.

SAFE ROTATED-SESSION RECOVERY (#1857): a live claim carrying this worker id but a different session
still refuses — the #419 twin guard is not weakened — but its diagnostic now names the marker session
and the `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`/`FSGG_WORKER` pin needed for a worker whose harness rotated sessions.
It explicitly warns not to mint a new id and strand the live claim.

Patch is not defensible: both changes alter caller-visible diagnostic/recovery behavior on a 0.x tool.
No Protocol.fs wire-surface change is included.

PREVIOUS RELEASE NOTES (0.16.0):

EIGHT merged engine commits since 0.15.0 across 32 source/project files. The #1900 squash commit
consolidates four reviewed input commits, making eleven pre-merge inputs in total. Protocol.fs moved,
so the generated worker contract and the published engine were out of step until this release.

FOLLOW-UP QUEUES AND THE DONE BOUNDARY (.github#1900/#1952): `followup audit` now observes every local
queue, previews reconciliation, and applies safe removal only after a durable GitHub disposition is
verified. `done` audits residual promises before stamping completion. `--apply` is now valid for the
followup command, and the JSON/text result vocabulary gains the audit and reconciliation outcomes.

FAIL-CLOSED MARKER SCANS (.github#1896/#1949): scheduler selection, claim/adopt/reap checks, and active
touch-set decisions no longer continue from a partial comment scan. A previously successful operation
may now refuse with an incomplete/undetermined result rather than silently ignoring a hidden claim.

SCHEDULING AND RANKING: BLOCKER-CLEARED respects an in-flight implementation (.github#1738/#1921);
decision items stay out of the worker queue (.github#1887/#1946); Severity is an ordered rank axis
(.github#1901); and `lint` names consolidation candidates when overlapping work is one cause
(.github#1915).

SKILL AND KIT DIAGNOSTICS: skill views are derived from receiver declarations (.github#1948), and KIT
DIGEST gives correct claim advice for skill files (.github#1942).

KNOWN RESIDUE: .github#1892 still decides whether secondary rate limits receive a distinct exit code or
remain EX_RATE with richer data. .github#1857 still tracks a session id rotating mid-item and stranding
the worker's own heartbeat/release/done operations. Neither behavior is claimed fixed by this release.
</PackageReleaseNotes>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!-- RestorePackagesWithLockFile, RestoreLockedMode and DisableImplicitLibraryPacksFolder (ADR-0006,
         ADR-0032) are INHERITED from the root Directory.Build.props, which imports the org-shared
         config this repo authors and ships. Every project here used to set them by hand because
         .github did not consume its own build config (#609). It does now. -->

    <!-- The engine is invoked once per scheduling call, on every worker, in a fan-out. Startup cost
         is the whole of its runtime cost, so it takes no dependency it does not need. No
         Spectre.Console here: JSON is the contract, and the text projection is a handful of printfn. -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="Json.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Json.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Snapshot.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Options.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Identity.fs" />
    <Compile Include="RefParsing.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="RefParsing.fs" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="FollowupAudit.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Chores.fs" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="KitDigest.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Render.fs" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="ReadyApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="LintApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="BoardFactsApplication.fs" />
    <!-- AFTER `Client.fs`, deliberately: the follow-up queue reuses `parseRefIn` and the exit constants
         rather than growing a second answer to "what is a ref?" or "what does an empty look exit?" (#485).
         It is a LOCAL verb — no token, no board — and depends on `Client` for those two things only. -->
    <Compile Include="Followups.fsi" />
    <Compile Include="Followups.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Client.fs" />
    <Compile Include="SemanticDiffApplication.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.Core/FS.GG.Coord.Core.fsproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FS.GG.Coord.GitHub/FS.GG.Coord.GitHub.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="FSharp.Core" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
